Because the percentages are worked out separately for each column, the same function can be used
on columns with very different ranges, and zero will be white in all of them. (The range that the
percentages refer to also stretches to include any numeric stops, so a column with no negative
values is colored from white up to green, with no red at all.)

Repeating a stop makes a sharp change in color at that value, rather than a gradual one. Here,
decreases are shades of orange and increases are shades of purple, with no blending across zero:

::: {#ec7a5bef .cell execution_count=14}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(
    GT(changes_df)
    .data_color(
        columns="change",
        fn=col_numeric(
            palette=["#E66101", "#FDB863", "#B2ABD2", "#5E3C99"],
            stops=["0%", 0, 0, "100%"],
        ),
    )
    .fmt_number(columns="change", decimals=1, force_sign=True)
)
```

